;;; Strict.mon --- Immediate evaluation strategy

:author   Laluxx
:version  0.3.0
:keywords strict eager identity evaluation strategy

;;; Commentary:

 Strict is the immediate evaluation strategy. It is the operational identity
 strategy: a Strict value contains an already evaluated value, and deferring a
 callback under Strict executes that callback immediately.

 Strict exists as a first-class type constructor even though the compiler may
 erase it completely. The type-level constructor is what lets structural Core
 abstractions be parameterized uniformly by evaluation strategy:

   Spine Strict a
   Tree  Strict a

 can share the same structural definitions as their Lazy counterparts without
 duplicating algorithms or laws.

 ;;; Mathematical Model

 Extensionally, Strict is naturally isomorphic to Identity:

                      Strict A  ≅  A

 with

                      defer k   = StrictValue (k ())
                      demand    = inverse of StrictValue

 Thus Strict contributes no delayed computational structure. Its purpose is to
 make immediate evaluation explicit in types whenever a shape is parameterized
 by evaluation strategy.

 Strict is shallow and local to the wrapped computation. It does not recursively
 force arbitrary payload fields and it is not a global "deep strictness" marker.
 In a structure such as =Tree Strict a=, only recursive positions explicitly
 wrapped by Strict are governed by this strategy.

 The compiler may erase Strict wrappers after type checking, provided doing so
 preserves quantities and all observable behavior.

 Every inhabited Strict value is already evaluated, so its demand is trivially
 total. =total-strict= exposes that fact as Control.Evaluation.TotalDemand
 evidence without traversing or copying the payload.

 =Strict= is an explicit evaluation strategy, not the result of strictness
 analysis. A future optimizer may prove that a computation stored under Lazy is
 necessarily demanded and evaluate it earlier, but that theorem is separate from
 the source-level fact that a recursive position was declared Strict. Keeping the
 two notions distinct avoids confusing programmer-visible evaluation structure
 with a compiler analysis of variable usage.

 ;;; Research Basis

 Paul Blain Levy, "Call-by-Push-Value: A Subsuming Paradigm", TLCA 1999.
   https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-48959-2_17

 Daniel Sainati, Joseph W. Cutler, Benjamin C. Pierce, and Stephanie Weirich,
 "Typing Strictness", POPL 2026.
   https://doi.org/10.1145/3776657

 Levy motivates making the value/computation boundary explicit. The more recent
 strictness work is relevant to future optimization because it distinguishes
 intensional variable usage from the older extensional notion of strictness.
 Monad's explicit Strict wrapper is neither analysis: it is source-level timing
 structure against which such optimizations must be justified.

;;; Code:

import Control.Evaluation
import Control.Applicative

module Control.Strict
  [Strict StrictValue strict run-strict total-strict]


data Strict a
  = StrictValue a


define strict :: a -> Strict a
  value -> StrictValue value


define run-strict :: Strict a -> a
  [StrictValue value] -> value

╭─ A Strict value is already evaluated, so every demand is definitionally total.
define total-strict :: Strict a -> TotalDemand Strict a
  [StrictValue value] -> total-pure value


instance Functor Strict
  map f [StrictValue value] -> StrictValue (f value)


instance Applicative Strict
  pure value -> StrictValue value
  applyA [StrictValue f] [StrictValue value] -> StrictValue (f value)


instance Evaluation Strict
  defer thunk -> StrictValue (thunk ())
  demand value -> run-strict value


tests
  :values:
  assert-eq (run-strict (strict 7)) 7 "Strict unwraps its value"
  :values:
